.TH rift 1  "rift 0.1.0" 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
rift \- Diff text files of any size under a hard memory limit
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrift\fR [\fB\-\-summary\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\fR] [\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-\-stats\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIA\fR> <\fIB\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBrift\fR compares two text files of any size, from megabytes to
hundreds of gigabytes, under a heap limit set with \fB\-\-mem\fR. It
writes a unified diff that \fBpatch\fR(1) applies to \fIA\fR to get
\fIB\fR. Its other modes print a summary, open a terminal view, or
compare records by key.
.PP
Rift hashes every line once and cuts the pair into segments at lines that
occur once in each file. Segments with identical bytes are equal. The
others are refined on demand.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Print line counts, the segment map and resource use instead of a diff
.TP
\fB\-\-mem\fR \fI<MEM>\fR [default: 256M]
Heap limit, in bytes or with a K, M, G or T unit (powers of 1024)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Browse the diff in the terminal instead of printing it
.TP
\fB\-\-stats\fR
After a diff, print peak heap, time and approximate regions to standard error
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Compare records by key instead of lines, regardless of row order: csv:N (column, from 1), csv:NAME (header), json:PATH (dotted field) or copy:NAME (COPY column of a Postgres dump)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIA\fR>
First file
.TP
<\fIB\fR>
Second file
.SH MODES
.TP
\fBrift\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIA\fR \fIB\fR
Writes a unified diff to standard output, as \fBdiff \-u\fR does.
\fBpatch\fR(1) applied to \fIA\fR gives \fIB\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Writes no diff. Prints the line and byte count of each file, the number
of segments that are equal, differing or approximate, the first 10
differing ranges (counted from 0, end excluded), the moved blocks, the
peak heap and the time.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR, \fBrift tui\fR \fIA\fR \fIB\fR
Opens a terminal view of the diff. The view is usable before the diff is
complete. A hunk still being refined shows as one pending row and fills
in by itself. The keys are:
.RS
.TP
\fBn\fR, \fBp\fR
Jump to the next and previous difference.
.TP
\fBspace\fR, \fBb\fR
Page down and up.
.TP
\fBg\fR, \fB:\fR
Go to a line of \fIA\fR.
.TP
\fBm\fR
Jump from the first moved block on screen to its other end.
.TP
\fBq\fR
Quit.
.RE
.IP
\fBrift tui\fR is \fBrift \-i\fR. With fewer than two arguments after
\fBtui\fR, it is a file name.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fISPEC\fR
Compares records by key and ignores their order. Prints one line per
key that differs, with tabs between the fields and line numbers counted
from 1. \fB~\fR marks a key in both files with different records, \fB\-\fR a key only in
\fIA\fR, \fB+\fR a key only in \fIB\fR. \fIA\fR and \fIB\fR are the line where the record starts,
or \fB\-\fR when it is absent:
.RS
.nf
~\fIKEY\fR	\fIA\fR	\fIB\fR
\-\fIKEY\fR	\fIA\fR	\-
+\fIKEY\fR	\-	\fIB\fR
.fi
.RE
.IP
\fISPEC\fR is one of:
.RS
.TP
\fBcsv:\fIN\fR
CSV column \fIN\fR, from 1.
.TP
\fBcsv:\fINAME\fR
CSV column with the header \fINAME\fR.
.TP
\fBjson:\fIPATH\fR
Dotted field path in NDJSON records, such as \fBuser.id\fR.
.TP
\fBcopy:\fINAME\fR
Column \fINAME\fR of the \fBCOPY\fR blocks of a \fBpg_dump\fR file.
.RE
.IP
The key is printed as the file has it, quotes included. A tab, line
break or backslash in a key prints as \fB\\t\fR, \fB\\n\fR or
\fB\\\\\fR. A record counts as unchanged only after its bytes compare
equal. A key that occurs twice in one file stops the run with exit status
2. \fB\-\-key\fR cannot be combined with \fB\-\-summary\fR or
\fB\-\-interactive\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-stats\fR
After a diff, prints to standard error the peak heap, the time, the
number of approximate regions and the moved blocks. With \fB\-\-key\fR it
prints the peak heap, the time, the number of passes over the files and
the number of records in each file.
.TP
\fB\-\-mem\fR \fISIZE\fR
Sets the heap limit. The default is 256M. A limit below about 12 MiB is
refused with a budget error. In key mode a larger limit means fewer
passes over the files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit status follows \fBdiff\fR(1).
.TP
\fB0\fR
The files are identical.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The files differ.
.TP
\fB2\fR
An error: an unreadable file, a budget too small, a duplicate key or an
invalid option. The message goes to standard error.
.SH MEMORY
The heap stays at or under \fB\-\-mem\fR. Rift reserves each allocation
on the data path from the budget before it makes it, and reads files with
\fBpread\fR(2) into fixed buffers. It does not map files into memory. The
test suite checks the peak with a counting allocator.
.PP
These are not guaranteed:
.IP \(bu 2
The resident set size. It also holds the binary, thread stacks and the
page cache the kernel counts against the process.
.IP \(bu 2
A minimal diff. Like patience diff, the output can show a change as
larger than the smallest possible one.
.IP \(bu 2
Detection of every moved block. Blocks are found from sampled lines, so
some moves are missed.
.SH APPROXIMATE REGIONS
When a region is too large to refine within the budget, Rift replaces it
whole: the output is still correct, but coarse. This happens in large
regions with no line that is unique in both files, such as long runs of
identical lines.
.PP
The unified diff marks such a hunk with the word \fBapprox\fR after the
closing \fB@@\fR of its header, where \fBdiff \-p\fR prints a function
name. \fBpatch\fR(1) ignores it. The terminal view draws the hunk in
yellow with a \fB~\fR. \fB\-\-stats\fR counts the approximate segments
and lines.
.SH MOVED BLOCKS
A block of at least 3 lines that appears in both files at different
positions is reported as moved. The unified diff still shows it as a
deletion and an insertion. \fB\-\-summary\fR and \fB\-\-stats\fR list the
blocks, and the \fBm\fR key in the terminal view jumps to the other end
of one. When the budget leaves no room for the search, the output says so.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a patch and apply it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift a.sql b.sql > changes.patch
patch a.sql changes.patch
.fi
.RE
.PP
Browse the differences:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-i a.sql b.sql
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count the differences without writing a diff, with a 1 GiB heap limit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-summary \-\-mem 1G a.csv b.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write a patch and report the peak heap and time on standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-stats a b > changes.patch
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare two CSV files by the column \fBid\fR, whatever the row order:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-key csv:id a.csv b.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare NDJSON by a nested field, and a \fBpg_dump\fR by a COPY column:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-key json:user.id a.ndjson b.ndjson
rift \-\-key copy:id a.sql b.sql
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBdiff\fR(1), \fBpatch\fR(1)
